course: biology
lesson: 12
title: Forests: woodland through the year and the great northern forest
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A wood changes all year like a house with four different rooms: bluebells in spring before the leaves close overhead, deep green shade in summer, red and gold leaves in autumn, bare branches and sleeping animals in winter.
explain: Kick through autumn leaves and you are walking on next year's soil: fungi and tiny creatures rot the leaves and dead wood back into food for the trees.
explain: Every animal has its own trick for the cold months: the squirrel buries acorns, the hedgehog sleeps through winter in a nest of leaves, the owl keeps hunting in the dark.
explain: Far to the north lies the biggest forest on land, the taiga, a dark green belt of spruce, pine, fir and larch across Canada, Scandinavia and Russia, where snow slides off cone-shaped trees and moose wade in the lakes.
fact: In autumn a tree stops making green chlorophyll and breaks it down, so the yellow and orange colours hidden underneath show through; red colours are made fresh in autumn.
fact: A tree grows one new ring of wood each year, light spring wood and darker summer wood; wide rings mean good years and thin rings mean hard dry years.
fact: Grey squirrels bury nuts in many separate spots and find many by memory and smell, but the acorns they and jays forget can grow into new oak trees.
fact: Bluebells grow from bulbs and flower in April and May, before the trees above are fully in leaf, because that is when sunlight still reaches the forest floor; about half of all the world's bluebells grow in Britain.
fact: A great spotted woodpecker drums on dead wood up to about 20 times a second to mark its territory, and its very long tongue wraps around the back of its skull.
fact: The taiga, or boreal forest, is mostly spruce, pine, fir and larch, and it is the biggest land habitat on Earth, nearly a third of all the world's forest.
fact: Narrow waxy needles lose little water and survive frost, and the cone shape of a spruce lets heavy snow slide off instead of breaking the branches.
fact: The moose, called elk in Britain, is the largest deer; a big bull can stand over 2 metres at the shoulder and can put its whole head under water to eat salty water plants.
fact: A mushroom is only the fruit of a fungus; the main fungus is a web of fine threads in wood and soil that rots dead things back into soil, and some wild mushrooms are deadly, so never touch or eat them.
fact: Hedgehogs feed up in autumn, then hibernate in a nest of leaves, and their heart slows from about 190 beats a minute to about 20; a hedgehog wandering about in winter snow has woken too early and may be in trouble.
fact: The twit-twoo call is usually two tawny owls: one calls kee-wick and another answers hoo-hooo; owls cannot move their eyes, so they turn their heads, up to about 270 degrees.
fact: Lichen, like old man's beard, is not a plant but a fungus and a tiny green alga living together; beard lichens take water and food straight from the air, so they grow only where the air is very clean, and reindeer dig under snow to eat lichen.
exercise: Why do leaves turn yellow and orange in autumn? | The tree breaks down green chlorophyll and hidden colours show | Frost paints new colours onto the leaves during the cold autumn nights | The leaves get sunburnt over the summer | The tree is dying and going rusty
exercise: How many rings does a tree grow in one year? | One ring, light spring wood and darker summer wood | One ring each month, so twelve rings in every year | One ring every ten years | A new ring after every rain
exercise: What does a very thin tree ring tell you? | That year was hard, perhaps cold or dry | The tree was very young and small then | It was an extra warm, wet year with plenty of sun | The tree was cut that year
exercise: What happens to the acorns a squirrel forgets? | Some grow into new oak trees | They turn into soil the same week | The squirrel's babies always find them | They stay as acorns for a hundred years
exercise: Why do bluebells flower in April and May? | Light still reaches the forest floor before the leaves close | Bees only exist in spring and die out by the start of June | They need frost to open their flowers | The trees water them most in spring
exercise: Why does a woodpecker drum on a dead branch? | To mark its territory and talk to other woodpeckers | Because its beak itches in spring | To scare the trees into dropping their fruit and nuts | To sharpen its beak for the long winter ahead, when wood is frozen hard
exercise: What is the taiga mostly made of? | Spruce, pine, fir and larch | Oak, beech and maple trees | Palm trees and ferns | Bamboo and banana plants
exercise: Why does snow slide off a spruce tree? | Its cone shape and pointing branches let the snow slip off | Its needles are always warm and melt the snow as it lands | Spruce trees shake themselves in the wind on purpose to drop it | Its bark is covered in oil
exercise: How big is the taiga? | The biggest land habitat, nearly a third of all forest | About the size of one big city park | Smaller than the Amazon rainforest by far, about half its size | It covers only Scotland
exercise: Why does a moose put its head under the lake? | To eat water plants with salt and minerals it needs | To drink, because the lake is too shallow | To look for fish to catch and eat | To clean the mud off its big antlers after a fight with another moose
exercise: What is a mushroom? | The fruit of a fungus whose threads are in the wood and soil | A green plant that makes its food from sunlight, like grass | A seed that fell from a tree | A kind of lichen that grew too big
exercise: What does a hibernating hedgehog's heart do? | Slows from about 190 to about 20 beats a minute | Stops completely until spring, then starts again | Speeds up to keep it warm through the winter | Beats exactly the same as in summer
exercise: Who makes the twit-twoo you hear at night? | Usually two tawny owls calling to each other | One owl saying both halves, one after the other | A woodpecker drumming in the dark | A fox barking in the trees
exercise: How far can a tawny owl turn its head? | Up to about 270 degrees | A full circle, 360 degrees, and more | Only a little, like a person | It cannot turn its head at all
exercise: What is lichen? | A fungus and a tiny green alga living together | A moss that grows only on the bark of old trees | Old spider webs covered in dust | The dried-up roots of a tree
exercise: What does a lot of beard lichen in a forest tell you? | The air there is very clean | The trees there are very ill | The forest is about to burn | It rains there only once a year
exercise: What do reindeer eat under the snow in winter? | Lichen | Pine cones | Snow only | Bluebell bulbs
test: Granddad says leaves go red because frost paints them. right? | No, the green is broken down and red colours are made fresh | Yes, frost colours the leaves overnight | Yes, without frost the leaves would stay green all through the winter | No, the rain washes the green away
test: Ella counts 47 rings on a tree stump. About how old was the tree when it was cut? | About 47 years | About 470 years | About 4 years | About 94 years
test: What would happen if no squirrel or jay ever forgot an acorn? | Fewer new oaks would grow far from the old trees | Oak trees would grow much faster than before | Squirrels would starve in winter | Nothing, oaks never grow from acorns, only from cuttings
test: A beech wood is dark all summer. Why are the bluebells already finished by then? | They flower early while light still reaches the ground | Summer is too cold for them | Squirrels eat all the flowers in June and dig up the bulbs | They only flower every other year
test: In a cartoon, an owl spins its head round and round like a wheel. What is wrong? | Owls turn their heads only up to about 270 degrees | Nothing, owls can spin their heads round for ever | Owls cannot turn their heads at all | Owls turn their eyes, not their heads, like we do
test: oi found a massive mushroom in the woods, gonna fry it for tea | No, never eat wild mushrooms, some are deadly | Yes, big ones are always safe to eat | Yes, if it smells nice it is fine | Only if a squirrel has bitten it first
test: Sam saw a hedgehog wandering about in January in the snow. What is likely? | It has woken from hibernation and is in trouble, so tell an adult or rescue group | Hedgehogs always go for walks in winter | It is looking for flowers to eat | It is migrating south for the winter
test: A moose has 4 legs, 2 ears and 1 heart. How many legs do 3 moose have? | 12 | 8 | 6 | 16
test: My mate says the taiga is a tropical jungle full of monkeys. right? | No, it is the cold northern forest of spruce, pine and larch | Yes, it is in the middle of Africa, near the equator | Yes, it is the Amazon's other name | No, it is a hot, dry desert full of camels and cactuses
test: Why would beard lichen disappear from a forest near a new smoky factory? | It takes water and food straight from the air, so dirty air kills it | Factories cut down all the lichen for paper | The noise scares it away | Lichen only grows in the dark, and the factory lights shine on it all night long
test: In a game, a woodpecker gets a headache after pecking. In real life, why can it drum all day? | Its body is built for it and it drums for its living | It only pecks once a year | It wears soft moss on its beak like a cushion | It pecks very slowly so that it does not hurt its head at all
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the leaves of deciduous trees acquire yellow and orange hues in autumn. | The decomposition of chlorophyll reveals pigments previously concealed | The action of frost deposits new pigments upon the surface of the leaf | The leaves are scorched by the accumulated summer sunshine | The leaves absorb colour from the surrounding soil
exam: Ascertain the approximate age of a tree whose felled trunk exhibits sixty annual rings. | Approximately sixty years | Approximately six years | Approximately six hundred years | Approximately one hundred and twenty years
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the boreal forest, hereinafter the taiga? | It is the most extensive terrestrial habitat, dominated by coniferous trees | It is a tropical forest situated at the equator | It is composed principally of oak and beech | It is the smallest of the forests of the northern hemisphere, confined to a single country
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the hibernation of the hedgehog. | The rate of the heartbeat diminishes from about 190 to about 20 per minute | The heart ceases to beat entirely until the spring | The heartbeat is accelerated to generate warmth | The hedgehog remains active throughout the winter but abstains from food of every kind
